  "copies": [
    {
      "headline": "Noah's Backfield Meets John's Air Force",
      "body": "Noah's team name is a threat and his backfield delivers on it \u2014 Achane and Cook combine for 33.2, the single biggest edge in this matchup, and if either of them pops a long touchdown against these defenses this one flips fast. But everywhere else John just has more firepower. Puka Nacua alone nearly outscores Noah's whole receiver room, and with Brian Thomas Jr. on the other side John's 31.3 to 26.1 at wide receiver is real and it's spacious. Throw in Brock Bowers and Trey McBride occupying both his TE and flex slots and John is getting stars at spots where Noah is starting Rhamondre Stevenson.\nThat's the whole game: Noah needs his backs to outrun the math, because the perimeter positions are a mismatch. John's a step slow out of the gate historically, but he's 10-4 since figuring it out, and he's spent four seasons chasing the ring that his little brother Peter already has sitting at home. Noah's the postseason assassin nobody drafts seriously in round one \u2014 but round one is over, and Week 1 doesn't care about January legend."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chase Versus Jeanty Decides Week One",
      "body": "Kaleb's the reigning worst-to-first Cinderella, though these days he's just Mr. 8-6, three years running, and there's something fitting about him opening 2026 as a 2-point favorite instead of a double-digit underdog for once. Isaac's still chasing that 11-3 ghost from 2022, and this matchup is basically a coin flip dressed up as a math problem. Ashton Jeanty and Breece Hall give Kaleb a 7-point cushion at running back that Isaac's Javonte Williams and Cam Skattebo can't quite close, especially with Skattebo's role in a crowded Giants backfield still a little unstable. Justin Herbert also just outclasses Bo Nix by nearly three points before you even factor volatility.\nBut Isaac punches back hard at receiver. Ja'Marr Chase alone is nearly as many points as Kaleb's whole tight end situation, and pairing him with Malik Nabers and Terry McLaurin gives Isaac a six-point wide receiver edge that eats most of Kaleb's backfield advantage. The Lions defense adds another cushion nobody's talking about. This one comes down to whether Jeanty's touches outrun Chase's targets, and on paper, Kaleb's 2.1-point edge feels like it's living dangerously close to a Chase explosion."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Grant's Receivers Try To Outrun Burrow",
      "body": "Grant's whole case here is the wideout room, and it's not close. Amon-Ra St. Brown and Zay Flowers pair with Waddle in the flex to put up 33 through the top two plus 12.2 more, a full station wagon of points ahead of Rashee Rice and Egbuka. That gap, plus Dallas Goedert's mild TE edge, is basically the margin of victory on paper. CJ counters with the safer floor everywhere else \u2014 Burrow over Mayfield by two, Jonathan Taylor grinding against a lighter Colts slate, and Fairbairn plus a Steelers defense that should feast. It's stars-and-scraps against balanced-and-boring, and balanced-and-boring usually needs the stars to slip.\nFitting opener for two guys who've lived on opposite ends of the stability spectrum. Grant is the walking variance report, 3-11 to 9-5 to 5-9, nobody less predictable in this league. CJ has been quietly stuck under .500 for three of four years, the definition of solid-but-unspectacular. Week 1 splits the difference on paper, 120.2 to 115.3, close enough that Amon-Ra having a normal Sunday probably decides the whole thing."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rezac's Backfield Bullies Reigning Champ Early",
      "body": "Logan opens his post-dress redemption tour with the best RB room either of these guys has fielded in years \u2014 Saquon Barkley walking into a Dallas defense that got gouged on the ground all last season, plus Jeremiyah Love as a receiving-down joker who profiles as an every-week 15-plus. That duo alone outscores Adam's Kenneth Walker/Bucky Irving tandem by 6.6, and Jalen Hurts tacks on another 4-plus over Jayden Daniels. Add a Chargers defense that should feast, and the math says Logan should be comfortable covering the number even before garbage time.\nAdam's counter lives at receiver, where Jaxon Smith-Njigba and Chris Olave give him more firepower than Justin Jefferson and Tetairoa McMillan on paper, and Sam LaPorta's a real weekly edge over Isaiah Likely. But receiver corps don't win 8-point spreads, running back rooms do. Adam's the gold standard of this league, 34-22 for a reason, but he's never once lost to the guy who's spent two straight seasons choking as the 1 seed \u2014 and Logan would love nothing more than to open the year proving that curse only applies in January."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Kittle's Zero Bagel Costs The Aura Farm",
      "body": "Peter opens his post-13-1-collapse era the same way he's opened most seasons since: with a roster that looks better on paper than his recent finishes suggest. Lamar gives him a clean edge at QB, Bijan is the best player in this game by himself, and Tucker Kraft turning in a real tight end line while Christian rolls out George Kittle at a flat zero is close to disqualifying on its own. That TE gap alone is nearly the whole projected margin. Christian's counter lives at receiver, where CeeDee Lamb and DeVonta Smith outscore Peter's AJ Brown-Pickens pairing by four and a half, and Chase Brown plus Etienne give him just enough juice on the ground to keep RB roughly even.\nAdd it up and Christian's actually winning three of seven categories, he's just losing the two biggest ones by a mile. That's been the Aura Farm story since 2022 \u2014 boom-or-bust, never irrelevant, never actually stable. Peter, still chasing whatever version of himself went 13-1 that first year, needs Kraft and Bijan to hold since Jonathon Brooks and Tee Higgins are shakier than they look. On paper this is Peter's to lose, and Christian needs Kittle to at least show up alive."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gibbs Is the Difference in Week 1",
      "body": "Leif rolls into Week 1 with the flashier top of the roster and it's not close \u2014 Josh Allen edges Jaxson Dart, and Christian McCaffrey plus Derrick Henry is as good a one-two RB punch as anyone drafted this year on paper. But Mitchell built for volume everywhere else, and it shows. Jahmyr Gibbs alone nearly matches Leif's entire backfield, and when you tack on Josh Jacobs against a Green Bay-adjacent floor and Nico Collins as a true WR1, the receiver and tight end rooms just have more juice. Kyle Pitts remains a tease; Tyler Warren doesn't have that problem.\nThe margins are thin across the board \u2014 Mitchell wins four of seven positional battles by less than two points each \u2014 which tracks for the human coin flip who's finished 7-7 three times in four years and still somehow found two playoff wins in there. Leif, meanwhile, is four years deep into the Joey Lunchbox bit, and a 22-34 record doesn't lie. Projections have this at 122.6 to 117.0, close enough that a Jason Myers miss or a Broncos garbage-time score decides it."
    }
  ],
  "intro": "Four champions in four years and the league opens 2026 no closer to figuring out who's actually good. The one worth watching is Adam at Logan \u2014 the reigning champ who finally broke the 1-seed curse walking into the building of a guy who's worn the dress once and taken zero playoff wins across two top seeds, and Vegas-by-projection likes Logan by 8. Noah, meanwhile, is doing Noah things: proj underdog in Week 1 against John, because his regular season doesn't start mattering until January."
